/*
Idea:
Let d1(v)=A[v], d2(v)=B[v]. We need an undirected connected unweighted graph
with two distinguished vertices 1 and 2 such that these are exact distances.

Necessary conditions:
- A[1]=0, B[2]=0
- A[2]=B[1]=D where D = dist(1,2) >= 1 unless N=1
- For every vertex v: |A[v]-B[v]| <= D <= A[v]+B[v]
  (triangle inequalities with sources 1 and 2)

A classical characterization:
For each vertex v, define
  x = (A[v] - B[v] + D)/2
  y = (A[v] + B[v] - D)/2
Then x,y must be integers, 0<=x<=D, y>=0.
Interpretation: x is the position on a shortest path from 1 to 2 (the "spine"),
and y is the distance from that spine. Vertex v belongs to branch attached at spine
position x with depth y.

To realize all distances with minimum edges:
- Build the spine of length D: one representative vertex for each position 0..D.
  Positions 0 and D must be vertices 1 and 2 respectively.
- Every other vertex with y=0 must coincide with the unique spine vertex at position x.
  Therefore if there is any extra vertex with y=0, impossible.
- For each fixed x, vertices with y>0 only need a rooted tree hanging from spine node x.
  To minimize edges while realizing exact depths, for each x and each depth d>=1,
  all vertices of depth d can share one chain vertex except one actual graph vertex can
  occupy that position. Additional vertices at same (x,d) require extra leaves attached
  to some depth d-1 vertex, but then their depth would be d and x unchanged only if they
  attach within same branch level. The minimum number of edges for k vertices at depth d
  given there exists at least one vertex at depth d-1 is exactly k if depth d already
  has a continuation from previous levels? Better viewed incrementally:

  For each x, let cnt[d] = number of given vertices with that x and y=d (d>=1).
  To connect any depth-d vertices, we need at least one parent at depth d-1.
  Thus if cnt[d]>0 and there are no vertices/nodes at depth d-1, impossible.
  Minimum new edges contributed at depth d:
    - if cnt[d-1]>0 (there is at least one actual vertex at previous depth), each of cnt[d]
      vertices can attach directly to one previous-depth actual vertex => cnt[d] edges.
    - if cnt[d-1]==0 but some constructed node exists at depth d-1, that node would have to
      be an extra Steiner node, but Steiner nodes are not allowed: all graph vertices are exactly
      the N given vertices. Therefore every used intermediate node must correspond to some input
      vertex. Hence for feasibility, for every d>=1 with cnt[d]>0, we need cnt[d-1]>0, with
      cnt[0]=1 (the spine node).
  Consequently, depths for each x must form a prefix 1..mx with cnt[d]>=1.
  Then minimal edges for branch x are simply sum_d cnt[d], since every non-spine vertex needs
  exactly one edge to previous depth.

Total minimum edges = D (spine) + number of non-spine vertices = D + count(y>0).
This works iff all feasibility conditions above hold.

Also need uniqueness of vertex 1 and 2 labels:
- vertex 1 must have (A,B)=(0,D)
- vertex 2 must have (A,B)=(D,0)

Special case N=1 impossible because graph connected with vertices 1 and 2 referenced, so N>=2.
*/

public class Main {
    static class FastScanner {
        private final InputStream in;
        private final byte[] buffer = new byte[1 << 16];
        private int ptr = 0, len = 0;

        FastScanner(InputStream is) {
            in = is;
        }

        private int read() throws IOException {
            if (ptr >= len) {
                len = in.read(buffer);
                ptr = 0;
                if (len <= 0) return -1;
            }
            return buffer[ptr++];
        }

        long nextLong() throws IOException {
            int c;
            do c = read(); while (c <= ' ' && c != -1);
            long sgn = 1;
            if (c == '-') {
                sgn = -1;
                c = read();
            }
            long val = 0;
            while (c > ' ') {
                val = val * 10 + (c - '0');
                c = read();
            }
            return val * sgn;
        }

        int nextInt() throws IOException {
            return (int) nextLong();
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        FastScanner fs = new FastScanner(System.in);
        int N;
        try {
            N = fs.nextInt();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            return;
        }

        long[] A = new long[N + 1];
        long[] B = new long[N + 1];
        for (int i = 1; i <= N; i++) A[i] = fs.nextLong();
        for (int i = 1; i <= N; i++) B[i] = fs.nextLong();

        if (N < 2) {
            System.out.println(-1);
            return;
        }

        long D = A[2];

        // Basic source constraints
        if (A[1] != 0 || B[2] != 0 || B[1] != D || D <= 0) {
            System.out.println(-1);
            return;
        }

        // For each spine position x, record which positive depths appear.
        HashMap<Long, HashSet<Long>> depths = new HashMap<>();
        long nonSpine = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i <= N; i++) {
            long a = A[i], b = B[i];

            if (Math.abs(a - b) > D || D > a + b) {
                System.out.println(-1);
                return;
            }

            long numX = a - b + D;
            long numY = a + b - D;
            if ((numX & 1L) != 0 || (numY & 1L) != 0) {
                System.out.println(-1);
                return;
            }

            long x = numX / 2;
            long y = numY / 2;

            if (x < 0 || x > D || y < 0) {
                System.out.println(-1);
                return;
            }

            if (y == 0) {
                // Must be exactly the unique spine vertex at position x.
                // So only allowed for vertex 1 at x=0, vertex 2 at x=D, and at most
                // one vertex for each internal x.
                // Since every label is a distinct graph vertex, duplicates on spine impossible.
                // We'll count occurrences per x.
            } else {
                nonSpine++;
                depths.computeIfAbsent(x, k -> new HashSet<>()).add(y);
            }
        }

        // Check uniqueness on spine positions.
        HashMap<Long, Integer> spineCount = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 1; i <= N; i++) {
            long a = A[i], b = B[i];
            long x = (a - b + D) / 2;
            long y = (a + b - D) / 2;
            if (y == 0) {
                spineCount.put(x, spineCount.getOrDefault(x, 0) + 1);
                if (spineCount.get(x) > 1) {
                    System.out.println(-1);
                    return;
                }
            }
        }

        // Need actual vertex 1 at x=0 and vertex 2 at x=D already implied; internal spine
        // positions may or may not have a vertex. But if a branch uses depth >=2 at some x,
        // we need a depth-1 vertex, etc. Internal spine positions themselves are represented
        // by some graph vertex only if there is y=0 there; otherwise no such vertex exists.
        // However the spine path requires a vertex at every position 0..D.
        // Therefore every position 0..D must have exactly one y=0 vertex.
        for (long x = 0; x <= D; x++) {
            if (spineCount.getOrDefault(x, 0) != 1) {
                System.out.println(-1);
                return;
            }
        }

        // For each x, positive depths must form a prefix {1..mx}
        for (Map.Entry<Long, HashSet<Long>> e : depths.entrySet()) {
            HashSet<Long> set = e.getValue();
            long mx = 0;
            for (long d : set) mx = Math.max(mx, d);
            for (long d = 1; d <= mx; d++) {
                if (!set.contains(d)) {
                    System.out.println(-1);
                    return;
                }
            }
        }

        long answer = D + nonSpine;
        System.out.println(answer);
    }
}
